  <exercises xml:id="exercises-practiceexam5">
    <title>Questions</title> 

   <exercise>
  <statement>
    <p> Let <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> be the vector space of all real polynomials of degree at most <m>2</m>.
    Let <m>W</m> be the subset of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m> consisting of those polynomials <m>p</m>
    that satisfy the condition
    <me>p'(0) + p(1) = 0.</me>

    <ol>
      <li><p> What is the dimension of <m>W</m>? </p></li>
      <li><p> Find a basis for <m>W</m>.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p> Write a general polynomial <m>p(x) = ax^2 + bx + c</m>.
    Compute <m>p'(x)</m>, then evaluate <m>p'(0)</m> and <m>p(1)</m>.
    The given condition gives a linear equation relating <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p> The dimension of <m>W</m> is <m>2</m>.
    One possible basis for <m>W</m> is
    <me>\{x^2 - 1,\; x - 2\}.</me>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Let <m>p(x) = ax^2 + bx + c</m> be an arbitrary element of <m>\mathbb{P}_2</m>.
      Then <m>p'(x) = 2ax + b</m>, so
      <me>p'(0) = b.</me>
      Also,
      <me>p(1) = a + b + c.</me>
      The condition <m>p'(0) + p(1) = 0</m> gives
      <me>b + (a + b + c) = 0,</me>
      which simplifies to
      <me>a + 2b + c = 0.</me>
    </p>

    <p>
      Solving for <m>c</m>, we obtain
      <me>c = -a - 2b.</me>
      Therefore every polynomial in <m>W</m> has the form
      <me>
        p(x) = a(x^2 - 1) + b(x - 2).
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      This shows that <m>W</m> is spanned by the polynomials
      <m>x^2 - 1</m> and <m>x - 2</m>.
      Since these polynomials are linearly independent,
      they form a basis for <m>W</m>.
      Hence
      <me>\dim W = 2.</me>
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise> 

 <exercise>
  <statement>
    <p> Let <m>V</m> be the vector space of all real sequences
    <me>(x_1, x_2, x_3, \dots)</me>

    with addition and scalar multiplication defined componentwise.</p>

    <p>
    Define
    <me>
      W = \{ \mathbf{x} \in V : x_{n+1} - x_n \text{ is constant for all } n \ge 1 \}.
    </me>

    <ol>
      <li><p> Show that <m>W</m> is a subspace of <m>V</m>. </p></li>
      <li><p> Describe all sequences in <m>W</m> explicitly. </p></li>
      <li><p> Find a spanning set for <m>W</m> and determine the dimension of <m>W</m>. </p></li>
    </ol>
  </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p> If the difference <m>x_{n+1} - x_n</m> is constant, try rewriting
    <m>x_{n+1}</m> in terms of <m>x_n</m>.
    Think about how many initial values determine an entire sequence in <m>W</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
    The set <m>W</m> is a subspace of <m>V</m>.
    Every sequence in <m>W</m> has the form
    <me>x_n = a + (n-1)c</me>
    for real numbers <m>a</m> and <m>c</m>.
    A spanning set for <m>W</m> is
    <me>\{(1,1,1,\dots),(0,1,2,3,\dots)\}</me>
    and <m>\dim W = 2</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The zero sequence <m>(0,0,0,\dots)</m> satisfies
      <m>0 - 0 = 0</m> for all <m>n</m>, so <m>\mathbf{0} \in W</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Let <m>\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{y} \in W</m>.
      Then there exist constants <m>c</m> and <m>d</m> such that
      <m>x_{n+1} - x_n = c</m> and <m>y_{n+1} - y_n = d</m> for all <m>n</m>.
      Then
      <me>
        (x+y)_{n+1} - (x+y)_n
        = (x_{n+1}-x_n) + (y_{n+1}-y_n)
        = c + d,
      </me>
      which is constant. Hence <m>\mathbf{x} + \mathbf{y} \in W</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For any scalar <m>\alpha</m>,
      <me>
        (\alpha x)_{n+1} - (\alpha x)_n
        = \alpha(x_{n+1} - x_n)
        = \alpha c,
      </me>
      which is constant. Thus <m>\alpha \mathbf{x} \in W</m>.
      Therefore, <m>W</m> is a subspace of <m>V</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      If <m>\mathbf{x} \in W</m>, then there exists a constant <m>c</m> such that
      <m>x_{n+1} = x_n + c</m> for all <m>n</m>.
      Since we also have <m>x_{n}=x_{n-1} + c</m>, we also know that <md><mrow> x_{n+1} \amp = (x_{n-1} + c)+c </mrow> <mrow> \amp =x_{n-1}+2c.</mrow> </md>  Likewise, we have that <me>x_{n+1}=x_{n-2}+3c</me> and we conclude the pattern with 
      <me>x_n = x_1 + (n-1)c.</me>
      Hence every sequence in <m>W</m> is an <em>arithmetic</em> sequence.
    </p>

    <p>
      Any <m>\mathbf{x} \in W</m> can be written as
      <me>
        \mathbf{x}
        = x_1(1,1,1,\dots) + c(0,1,2,3,\dots).
      </me>
      Therefore,
      <me>
        W = \operatorname{span}\{(1,1,1,\dots),(0,1,2,3,\dots)\}.
      </me>
      These two sequences are linearly independent, so <m>\dim W = 2</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>


</exercises>

